   "source": "---\n## Cell 3 — What is Ada-DF?\n\n**Ada-DF** (Adaptive Dynamic Feature Fusion) is a modern FER approach that addresses one  \nof the core limitations of POSTER++ and similar methods: distribution mismatch between  \ntraining datasets and real-world deployment conditions.\n\n**The problem Ada-DF solves:**  \nDatasets like RAF-DB and AffectNet have systematically different face distributions —  \ndifferent camera angles, lighting conditions, age ranges, and cultural expression norms.  \nModels trained on one dataset often perform 5–15% worse on another. Ada-DF uses adaptive  \nweighting to learn which features are more transferable across datasets.\n\n**Architecture overview:**\n- A **ViT or ResNet** backbone for initial feature extraction\n- An **adaptive feature selector** that learns to weight different feature channels  \n  based on distribution statistics (mean, variance) computed per batch\n- A **dual-branch fusion** module: one branch captures global expression features,  \n  the second captures local facial region features (AU-aware)\n- **Distribution-aware loss**: combines standard cross-entropy with a feature  \n  distribution alignment term that reduces cross-dataset gap\n\n**Why include Ada-DF as a benchmark:**  \nIt represents the class of methods that go beyond single-dataset FER and attempt to  \nlearn more generalisable facial features — which is exactly the challenge EmpathBot  \nfaces (training on AffectNet + RAF-DB, deploying on live webcam video).\n\n> **Note:** If Ada-DF refers to a specific paper you have cited in your literature review,  \n> update the `ADADF_REPO_URL` and the model loading code in Cell 5 to match that paper's  \n> official implementation. The evaluation framework (Cells 8–14) remains identical."
